**Theorem 4.1 (explicit normalization witness).** Under the hypotheses
of Theorem 3.1, let $p$ be a positive prime other than $3$ and suppose
$p^2\mid 1+9a$. Then

$$
z=\frac{\theta^2}{p}
$$

is integral over $\mathbb Z$ but does not belong to
$A=\mathbb Z\cdot1+\mathbb Z\cdot\theta+
\mathbb Z\cdot(1+\theta+\theta^2)/3$. Consequently $A$ is a proper
subring of the full ring of integers of $K$.

Proof. Write $1+9a=p^2t$ with $t\in\mathbb Z$. Then
$z^3=(1+9a)^2/p^3=pt^2$, so $z$ satisfies a monic polynomial over
$\mathbb Z$. If $z=u+v\theta+w(1+\theta+\theta^2)/3$ with
$u,v,w\in\mathbb Z$, compare the coefficient of $\theta^2$ in the
rational power basis $(1,\theta,\theta^2)$. This gives $1/p=w/3$,
or $pw=3$, contradicting primality and $p\ne3$.
